@node Introduction
@chapter Introduction
@cindex experimental features
  Tessera X Gnus prepares context snapshots from Gnus articles.  A
snapshot contains article metadata and available body text in an
independent, read-only buffer.  It can be inspected or used by another
application.  The @samp{x} means experimental; context preparation is
the experiment provided by this version.

  The buffer in which you start preparation is the @dfn{source buffer}.
The @dfn{scope} is the set of articles selected for a snapshot.

  The commands work without enabling @code{tessera-gnus-mode}.  They do
not send language-model requests.  The snapshot format, size limits, and
commands for viewing and discarding snapshots are described in
@ref{Top,,, tessera-x, Tessera X}.

@node Getting Started
@chapter Getting Started

  Before preparing a context, make the packages available to Emacs and
configure Gnus Agent for the groups you intend to read.

@menu
* Requirements::        Required packages and loading libraries.
* First Context::       Preparing and viewing a snapshot.
@end menu

@node Requirements
@section Requirements
@cindex Gnus Agent
@cindex installation
  This package requires Emacs 30.1, Tessera 0.1.0, and Tessera X 0.1.0
or later.  Gnus is included with Emacs.  Tessera also requires Alert 1.2
or later.  For a source checkout, add @file{lisp/tessera/},
@file{lisp/tessera-x/}, and @file{lisp/tessera-x-gnus/} to
@code{load-path}.  Then load the library:

@lisp
(require 'tessera-x-gnus)
@end lisp

  All context construction requires @code{gnus-agent} to be enabled.
Groups must have Agent data for local retrieval; downloading missing
bodies additionally requires an agentized method.  Requiring this
library does not configure those facilities.  For Agent setup, see
@ref{Gnus Unplugged,,, gnus, The Gnus Manual}.

  Use @kbd{M-x customize-group @key{RET} tessera-gnus @key{RET}} to
configure the options documented here.  You can also load
@code{tessera-gnus} and set them in your initialization file before
loading the snapshot commands.

@node First Context
@section Preparing a First Context

  In a Gnus Summary buffer, move point to an article and run @kbd{M-x
tessera-x-gnus-prepare-context}.  By default, a missing body may be
downloaded into the Agent.  To restrict preparation to existing Agent
bodies, set the following before invoking the command:

@lisp
(require 'tessera-gnus)
(setq tessera-x-gnus-body-policy 'local-only)
@end lisp

  After completion, run @kbd{M-x tessera-x-show-context} in the Summary
buffer to inspect the result.  This configuration restricts body
retrieval; it does not make an incomplete Agent cache complete.
@xref{Body Retrieval}.

@node Selecting Articles
@chapter Selecting Articles

  Selection commands include articles hidden by thread folding.  They
retain Gnus marks and article read state.

@menu
* Work Articles::       Region, process marks, or article at point.
* Current Subthread::   The current message and its descendants.
@end menu

@node Work Articles
@section Work Articles
@cindex selection
@deffn Command tessera-x-gnus-prepare-context
Prepare Gnus work articles from the current Summary buffer and return a
@code{tessera-x-context}.  Use articles in the active region, if any;
otherwise use articles with process marks, or the article at point when
there are no such marks.  Signal a user error outside Summary or when no
articles are selected.
@end deffn

  Retrieval and publication run synchronously.  The returned request can
be ready or failed.  Selecting articles does not remove process marks,
open articles, or mark them read.  The command's network behavior is
controlled by @code{tessera-x-gnus-body-policy}.

@node Current Subthread
@section The Current Article and Its Replies
@cindex descendants
@cindex subthreads
@deffn Command tessera-x-gnus-prepare-subthread-context &optional @
  local-index
Prepare the article at point and its descendants.  With a non-@code{nil}
@var{local-index}, or interactively with a prefix argument such as
@kbd{C-u}, supplement loaded records from the current group's Agent
overview.  Return a context object.  Signal a user error outside Summary
or when there is no current article.
@end deffn

  The ordinary scope is the current Summary results, including folded
articles.  A descendant is a reply to the current article, or a reply to
one of its replies.  When @code{gnus-show-threads} is non-@code{nil},
Tessera follows Gnus's thread structure together with reply references
in article headers.  With threading disabled, it uses those references
alone.  Matching subjects do not by themselves establish a relationship.

  This command selects descendants, not every ancestor or sibling in the
conversation.  Its retrieval policy is the same as for work articles.

@cindex local index expansion
@cindex Agent overview
@defopt tessera-x-gnus-subthread-scope
The default subthread scope, initially @code{results}.  The alternative
@code{local-index} also consults the current group's Agent overview.  A
non-@code{nil} command argument requests expansion regardless of this
option.  There is no prefix argument for forcing @code{results} when the
option is @code{local-index}.
@end defopt

  The Agent overview is its locally stored list of article headers.
Expansion uses that list to find additional descendants in the current
group, retaining selected articles already in Summary without adding
duplicates.  It can include articles outside a Summary filter.  Missing
or stale overview data makes the selection incomplete.

  This operation does not search a remote server for unknown
descendants.  It expands the selection locally; subsequent body
retrieval may still download known articles when the body policy permits
it.

@node Today Contexts
@chapter Today Contexts

  The today command reads locally indexed articles for the current
calendar day.  Its scope comes from the Gnus buffer in which it is run.

@deffn Command tessera-x-gnus-prepare-today-context
Prepare today's local Agent articles in the native scope and return a
context object.  In Summary, use the current group.  In a Group buffer,
use the group or topic at point.  Signal a user error in unsupported
buffers or on a Group line that identifies neither a group nor a topic.
@end deffn

  The source buffer determines which groups supply the local records:

@table @asis
@item Summary buffer
@cindex today, Summary scope
The command reads the current group's Agent overview rather than
restricting itself to the loaded or visible Summary rows.

@item Group buffer
@cindex today, group and topic scopes
@cindex topics
A group line selects that one group.  A topic line selects the topic's
groups and its descendant topics' groups.  Duplicate groups are read
only once.
@end table

  Groups without an agentized method or a readable Agent overview
contribute no records.  Date selection includes local midnight and
excludes the following midnight.  Records without a usable date are
excluded.  The local overview records are sorted by date before
conversation grouping.

  A group or topic with no matching records produces a valid empty
snapshot.  An invalid line at point is a selection error instead.

@cindex offline retrieval
  The today command never downloads bodies or overview data, regardless
of @code{tessera-x-gnus-body-policy}.  It reads available Agent bodies;
missing bodies retain metadata and a content note.  It does not
synchronize the server or broaden the local index to fill gaps.

  The local day's boundaries are computed when the command runs.
@xref{Local Day Boundaries,,, tessera-x, Tessera X}.

@node Body Retrieval
@chapter Body Retrieval

  The Agent is the source of article bodies for all three commands.
Only selection and subthread preparation can request missing bodies.

@menu
* Download Policy::     Agent downloads and unavailable bodies.
* Metadata and Text: Metadata and Body Text.  Article headers and extracted content.
@end menu

@node Download Policy
@section Download Policy
@cindex body policy
@defopt tessera-x-gnus-body-policy
How work-article and subthread commands obtain bodies.  The initial
value @code{download} uses native synchronous Agent fetching for bodies
that are absent or empty.  The value @code{local-only} reads only
existing Agent bodies.  Today preparation always uses local-only
retrieval.
@end defopt

  Downloads are grouped by newsgroup.  When downloads make bodies
available in the current Summary group, native availability marks are
updated.  Read marks and process marks are retained.  Downloading does
not display the articles.  Emacs waits for Agent downloads to finish
before the command returns.

  Both Gnus options are ordinary user options.  A buffer-local binding
can restrict a particular source; use Customize or configuration for
global defaults.  Changes affect subsequent commands, not existing
snapshot buffers.

@cindex missing bodies
  An inaccessible Agent body becomes @samp{Body absent from local Agent}
unless a more specific download failure has already been recorded.
Failures to fetch a group's bodies are reported as content notes so that
other bodies can remain usable.  Existing metadata is retained.

  A valid snapshot may contain articles with header information but no
body text.  For common MIME extraction and budget notices, see
@ref{Missing Content,,, tessera-x, Tessera X}.

@node Metadata and Body Text
@section Metadata and Body Text
@cindex message metadata
  Items retain the full subject, date, From, available To and Cc fields,
Message-ID, References, and newsgroup provenance.  Labels combine
available registry marks, Gmail labels, and Keywords.  Reading raw mail
can add or refine address and label header metadata.

  Native inferred parents are printed separately from References.
Connected conversations are grouped without merging unrelated articles
that merely share a subject.  MIME extraction keeps quotes and
signatures, lists attachment names and types, and does not verify or
decrypt security parts.  @xref{Mail Bodies,,, tessera-x, Tessera X}.

@node Using the Result
@chapter Using the Result
@cindex snapshot completion
  A successful command publishes a hidden snapshot buffer and invokes
@code{tessera-x-context-ready-hook} in the source buffer.  Preparation
does not select the result window.  The source's
@code{tessera-x-current-context} refers to its latest successful result.
For showing, discarding, and retaining old snapshots, see @ref{Snapshot
Lifecycle,,, tessera-x, Tessera X}.

  No key bindings are installed by this experimental package.  Commands
can be invoked with @kbd{M-x}; bindings and consumers belong in user
configuration.  For the ready hook and a consumer example, see
@ref{Consuming Snapshots,,, tessera-x, Tessera X}.

@node Troubleshooting
@chapter Troubleshooting

  Check the Agent configuration and the selected groups or articles when
preparation fails or omits expected content.

@menu
* Agent Setup Problems::  Enabling and configuring Gnus Agent.
* Incomplete Scopes::     Articles missing from the local overview.
* Reporting Bugs::        Information to include in a bug report.
@end menu

@node Agent Setup Problems
@section Agent Setup Problems
@cindex Agent setup errors
  If preparation reports that the Agent is not enabled, configure Gnus
Agent before retrying.  If a download note says that the method is not
agentized, verify that group's native Agent configuration.  Selecting
@code{local-only} prevents fetching but cannot supply absent data.

@node Incomplete Scopes
@section Incomplete Scopes

  If replies are missing, distinguish the loaded-result scope from the
Agent overview scope.  Expansion cannot find records absent from that
local overview.  If today's result is empty, check the selected group or
topic, local dates, and overview availability before changing the body
policy; today ignores that policy's download setting.

@node Reporting Bugs
@section Reporting Bugs

  For failures involving the shared result hook or buffer lifetime, see
@ref{Troubleshooting,,, tessera-x, Tessera X}.  Report backend problems
at @url{https://github.com/brsvh/emacs-tessera/issues} with the Emacs
and package versions, command, Agent policy, and a minimal reproduction.

